A delightfully simple 2D game engine for the D programming language .
It's easy to set up, hackable, and comes with the essentials built in. Parin sits somewhere between a small library like raylib and a big engine like Godot .
It offers more direction than small libraries, but far less overhead than big engines.
Its main ideas are: Code-driven design : No engine-mandated architecture, so gameplay code can be structured however fits the game. Flexible abstraction : Garbage collection is available for convenience, with the option to drop to manual management or avoid it entirely. Modular foundation : Most of Parin is built on Joka , a portable utility library that can be used on its own (see this raylib example ). Batteries included : Sensible defaults for common needs (animation helpers, timers, etc.). Basic code example: import parin; // Called every frame while the game is running. bool hello ( float dt) {
    lockResolution( 320 , 180 );
    drawText( " Helloo!!! " , mouse); return false ;
} // Creates a main function that calls the given function. mixin runDemo ! hello; Pixel-perfect physics engine Flexible dialogue system Atlas-based animation library Efficient tile map structures Simple UI library (WIP) Includes extras like Box2D, Steamworks, ENet, and microui Support for Web, Windows, Linux, and macOS This section shows how to install Parin using DUB .
Create a new folder and run the following commands inside it: dub init -t parin
dub run If everything is set up correctly, a window will appear showing a simple message. Available starting templates: dub init -t parin -- basic
dub init -t parin -- entity Create a new folder and run the following to... Prepare the folder: git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/Kapendev/parin parin_package
./parin_package/scripts/prepare # Or: .\parin_package\scripts\prepare.bat Run the project: ./parin_package/scripts/run # Or: .\parin_package\scripts\run.bat # Or: ./parin_package/scripts/run ldc2 macos # Or: ./parin_package/...
